findings.recommendation:
Add the short-value case to the existing test (verified: passes on the current implementation, and it is the only thing that kills the hoist mutant):

// A value no longer than the width must still render: the guard lives INSIDE the overflow
// branch, so width 0 is only an error when the value actually overflows it. This case is what
// pins the evaluation order the rewrite had to preserve.
func Test_printDataCell_zeroWidthShortValueDoesNotError(t *testing.T) {
	cfg := makeRenderConfig(6, 5)
	cfg.view.ColsWidth[0] = 0
	s := makeRenderResult(6, 1)
	s.Result.Values[0][0] = sql.NullString{String: "", Valid: true}

	var buf bytes.Buffer
	assert.NoError(t, printDataCell(&buf, s, cfg, 0, 0),
		"width 0 errors only when the value overflows it; a short value must still render")
	assert.Equal(t, "  ", buf.String(), "a short value in a zero-width column renders as the bare +2 gap")
}

This also covers the task file's 'sql.NullString with Valid: false -> .String is ""' edge case, which is otherwise unexercised. Either add this test or delete the evaluation-order sentence from Test_printDataCell_zeroOrNegativeWidth's comment — do not leave the claim standing without the case. No mock changes; this suite has no mocks.
